<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich Joachim Stengel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1694">1694</a>–<a href="/years/1787">1787</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich Joachim Stengel (1694–1787) was a German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> architect who served the princes of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a> as their master builder. He shaped Saarbrücken as a Baroque residence, designing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwigskirche">Ludwigskirche</a>, the palace and the Ludwigsplatz ensemble, as well as churches, palaces and town plans across the Saar region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-joachim-stengel.jpg" alt="Friedrich Joachim Stengel" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich-Joachim_Stengel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Friedrich Joachim Michael Stengel was born on 29 September 1694 to a Protestant family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>. At 14 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> into the care of his mother's brother and studied at the Prussian Academy of Arts, learning fortification engineering, civil architecture, artillery and geometry. In 1712 he entered the military service of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> as a cadet and spent several months in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1715 to 1719 Stengel worked as a state surveyor for the duchies of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> and Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>. A survey in Saxe-Eisenach brought him into contact with Adolphus von Dalberg, and he went to Fulda as surveyor and building inspector until 1729. In 1733 he became court architect to Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-prince-of-nassau-usingen">Charles of Nassau-Usingen</a>, for whom he converted the Prinzenpalais and built the east and west wings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biebrich-palace">Biebrich Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiesbaden">Wiesbaden</a>.</p><p>In 1735 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-prince-of-nassau-saarbrucken">William Henry, Prince of Nassau-Saarbrücken</a>, asked him to report on the condition of Saarbrücken Castle, his intended residence. Stengel found it structurally unsound and in 1738 was commissioned to plan its rebuilding, completed in 1748. He moved to Saarbrücken in 1740, and with the prince turned the town into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> residence, drawing up building codes and planning new urban spaces, commercial buildings and houses across the principality. His works there include the Friedenskirche (1743–1751), the Basilica of St. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> (1754–1758) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwigskirche">Ludwigskirche</a> (1762–1775), and nearby Schloss Jägersberg in Neunkirchen. He also rebuilt Dornburg Castle for Joanna Elisabeth of Holstein-Gottorp between 1751 and 1758.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1761 Stengel became General Building Director and Real Chamber Councilor of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a>; he also presided over the Forest Chamber and directed the Saarbrücken orphanage, poorhouse and penitentiary until he retired in 1775. He married Katharina Hoffmann in 1720; she died in 1741, and a year later he married Klara Storch, first maid to Princess Charlotte Amalie of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a>. Their sons Johann Friedrich and Balthasar Wilhelm both became master builders like their father. Stengel died on 10 January 1787 at the age of 92 and was buried in Saarbrücken two days later.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Friedrich Joachim Stengel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 18th century Prince William Henry and his son Louis made Saarbrücken a Baroque residence, with buildings by <b>Friedrich Joachim Stengel</b> including the Ludwigskirche, and developed coal mining and ironworks.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau-saarbrucken">County of Nassau-Saarbrücken</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.97,12.08&amp;year=1754" aria-label="Zerbst on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4822 368 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Friedrich Joachim Stengel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5272 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5272 638h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.97,12.08&amp;year=1754">What was here in 1754? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
